1. <source>Mol. Cell. Biol.</source> <volume>15</volume>:<fpage>6064</fpage>&#x02013;6074.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">7565759</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib30"><citation citation-type="journal">Wigge, P.A., and J.V. Kilmartin. <year>2001</year>. The Ndc80p complex from <italic>Saccharomyces cerevisiae</italic> contains conserved centromere components and has a function in chromosome segregation. <source>J. Cell Biol.</source> <volume>152</volume>:<fpage>349</fpage>&#x02013;360.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11266451</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list><fn-group><fn id="fn1"><label>*</label><p>Abbreviations used in this paper: 3-AT, 3-amino-1,2,4-triazole&#x0003b; AIRK, aurora/Ipl1-related kinase&#x0003b; GFP, green fluorescent protein&#x0003b; GST, glutathione-<italic>S</italic>-transferase&#x0003b; INCENP, inner centromere protein&#x0003b; MBP, myelin basic protein&#x0003b; SC, synthetic complete&#x0003b; WCE, whole cell extracts.</p></fn></fn-group></back></article>